**Description of the Terraform Program:**

This Terraform configuration sets up an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) and creates a Route 53 record that points to the ELB. The Route 53 zone is named "primary" and the ELB is named "main". The configuration includes a provider block for AWS, an ELB resource, a Route 53 zone, and a Route 53 record that references the ELB's DNS name. All necessary resources are declared and configured with default values where appropriate.

---

**Terraform HCL Program:**

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_elb" "main" {
  name               = "main"
  availability_zones = ["us-west-2a", "us-west-2b", "us-west-2c"]
  security_groups    = ["sg-12345678"] # Replace with a valid security group ID
  listener {
    instance_port     = 80
    instance_protocol = "http"
    lb_port           = 80
    lb_protocol       = "http"
  }
}

resource "aws_route53_zone" "primary" {
  name = "example.com" # Replace with your actual domain name
}

resource "aws_route53_record" "elb" {
  zone_id = aws_route53_zone.primary.id
  name    = "main.example.com" # Replace with your desired subdomain
  type    = "A"
  alias {
    name   = aws_elb.main.dns_name
    zone_id = aws_elb.main.zone_id
    evaluate_target_health = true
  }
}
```

---

**Notes:**

- Replace `"example.com"` with your actual domain name.
- Replace `"sg-12345678"` with a valid security group ID in your AWS account.
- The ELB is configured with a basic HTTP listener on port 80. You can modify this as needed.
- The Route 53 record is an alias record that points to the ELB's DNS name. This ensures that the record always points to the correct ELB endpoint.

