**Description of the Terraform Program:**

This Terraform configuration provisions a CloudWatch Metric Alarm named "foobar" that monitors the "CPUUtilization" metric from the "AWS/EC2" namespace. It then creates a Route 53 Health Check that uses this CloudWatch Metric Alarm as its health check configuration. The health check is set to consider the instance healthy if the metric does not meet the threshold (i.e., if there is not enough information about the alarm). All resources are created in the "us-east-1" region.

---

**Terraform HCL Program:**

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm" "foobar" {
  alarm_name          = "foobar"
  comparison_operator = "GreaterThanOrEqualToThreshold"
  evaluation_periods  = "1"
  metric_name         = "CPUUtilization"
  namespace           = "AWS/EC2"
  statistic           = "Average"
  period              = 120
  threshold           = 70
  treat_missing_data  = "notBreaching"
  dimensions = {
    InstanceId = aws_instance.example.id
  }
}

resource "aws_instance" "example" {
  ami           = "ami-0c55b159cbfafe1f0" # Amazon Linux 2 AMI (us-east-1)
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags = {
    Name = "example-instance"
  }
}

resource "aws_route53_health_check" "foobar_health_check" {
  failure_threshold = 1
  inverted          = true
  request_interval  = 30
  resource_path     = "/"
  type              = "CLOUDWATCH_METRIC"
  cloudwatch_metric {
    alarm_name = aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm.foobar.alarm_name
  }
}
```

---

**Notes:**

- The `aws_instance.example` is a placeholder instance used to provide the `InstanceId` for the CloudWatch Metric Alarm. You may replace this with an actual EC2 instance ID or use a data source to fetch it if needed.
- The `treat_missing_data` is set to `"notBreaching"` to ensure that the alarm does not trigger if there is not enough data, which aligns with the requirement that the health check should consider the status as "Healthy" in such cases.
- The `inverted` flag in the Route 53 health check is set to `true` to invert the logic of the alarm, so that the health check is "Healthy" when the alarm is not in a breaching state.

