**Description of the Terraform Program:**

This Terraform configuration creates an AWS S3 bucket named "my_unique_bucket_name" with a public access control list (ACL) set to "public-read" to allow read access to all users. It also defines an S3 bucket policy that allows the `s3:GetObject` action to any principal (i.e., anyone on the internet) but only if the request includes a specific referer header. The policy is attached to the bucket to enforce this condition. The configuration includes a provider block for AWS, ensuring the correct region is used.

---

**Terraform HCL Code:**

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-east-1"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket" "unique_bucket" {
  bucket = "my_unique_bucket_name"
  acl    = "public-read"
}

resource "aws_s3_bucket_policy" "bucket_policy" {
  bucket = aws_s3_bucket.unique_bucket.bucket

  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = "*"
        Action = "s3:GetObject"
        Resource = "arn:aws:s3:::my_unique_bucket_name/*"
        Condition = {
          StringLike = {
            "aws:Referer" = "https://example.com/*"
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  })
}
```

---

**Notes:**

- The bucket name "my_unique_bucket_name" must be globally unique across AWS. You may need to modify it to ensure uniqueness in your AWS account.
- The `aws:Referer` condition in the policy allows access only from requests originating from `https://example.com/*`. You can adjust this to match your specific domain or domains.
- The `public-read` ACL grants read access to all users, which may not be suitable for production environments. Consider using bucket policies or IAM roles for more granular access control.

