**Description of the Terraform Program:**

This Terraform configuration creates an Amazon ElastiCache user and authenticates it using IAM. It sets up an IAM user with the necessary permissions to interact with ElastiCache, creates an ElastiCache user, and associates the IAM user with the ElastiCache user using an IAM authentication policy. The configuration includes a provider block for AWS, an IAM user, an IAM role, and an ElastiCache user with IAM authentication enabled.

---

**Terraform HCL Program:**

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_iam_user" "elasti_cache_user" {
  name = "elasti_cache_iam_user"
}

resource "aws_iam_user_policy_attachment" "elasti_cache_access" {
  user       = aws_iam_user.elasti_cache_user.name
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonElastiCacheFullAccess"
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "elasti_cache_role" {
  name = "elasti_cache_iam_role"
  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [{
      Effect = "Allow"
      Principal = { Service = "elasticache.amazonaws.com" }
      Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
    }]
  })
}

resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "elasti_cache_role_access" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.elasti_cache_role.name
  policy_arn = "arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AmazonElastiCacheFullAccess"
}

resource "aws_elasticache_user" "iam_user" {
  user_id         = "iam_user"
  engine          = "redis"
  access_string   = "on ~* +@all"
  passwords       = ["SecretPassword123!"]
  iam_user        = aws_iam_user.elasti_cache_user.name
  no_password_required = false
}
```

---

**Notes:**

- This configuration assumes you are using Redis as the ElastiCache engine.
- The `access_string` is set to allow full access (`+@all`), which is suitable for testing or development. In production, you should restrict access as needed.
- The IAM user is granted the `AmazonElastiCacheFullAccess` policy, which allows full access to ElastiCache resources.
- The ElastiCache user is created with a password and linked to the IAM user for IAM-based authentication.
- The `no_password_required` flag is set to `false` to enforce password authentication. If you want to use IAM-only authentication, you can set this to `true` and omit the `passwords` field.

